//! member: false
//!
//! kill — send a signal to processes, or list the signals.
//!
//! References consulted: POSIX.1-2024 `kill`; GNU coreutils 9.11 `kill
//! --help`, the info manual's `kill invocation` node, and black-box
//! runs of the GNU binary in throwaway containers (linux x86-64) and on
//! CI's macOS runner, under `LC_ALL=C`. **Which binary:** GNU
//! coreutils' own `kill`, built from the oracle tarball with
//! `--enable-install-program=kill` (coreutils does not install it
//! otherwise). Not a shell's builtin, which is what `kill` runs in
//! every shell, and not the `/usr/bin/kill` of procps or util-linux,
//! which is what most systems ship. Never GNU source, nor any other
//! `kill`'s. The signal names, numbers and operand rules are
//! `bore/signals.lu`'s.
//!
//! GNU's rules, each measured against 9.11:
//!
//! - **Three forms.** `kill [-s SIGNAL | -SIGNAL] PID...` sends;
//!   `kill -l [SIGNAL]...` converts; `kill -t [SIGNAL]...` prints a
//!   table. `-L` is `-t`, and `-n SIGNAL` is `-s SIGNAL`; GNU's help
//!   names neither. The long names are `--signal`, `--list` and
//!   `--table`.
//! - **`-SIGNAL`** is an argument whose first character after the `-`
//!   is an upper-case letter or a digit: the rest of the argument is
//!   the signal (`-KILL`, `-Kill`, `-SIGkill`, `-9`, `-137`). `-L` is
//!   the table instead, so no signal can be written starting with it.
//!   Inside a cluster such a character is no option at all (`-lK` is
//!   `invalid option -- K`, unquoted, GNU's own wording rather than
//!   getopt's), and a lower-case word is a cluster (`-term` is `-t`,
//!   then an invalid `-e`).
//! - **A digit form is the signal only as the FIRST argument.** Anywhere
//!   later, `-9` is process group 9 — and option reading stops there,
//!   which loses every operand that came before it: `kill 123 -9`
//!   signals group 9 and never touches 123, and `kill -l 5 -9` answers
//!   for `-9` alone. Kept, because it is what GNU does.
//! - **One signal, one mode.** A second signal, even the same one, is
//!   `'X': multiple signals specified`; a second `-l` or `-t` is
//!   `multiple -l or -t options specified`; a signal with either is
//!   `cannot combine signal with -l or -t`, said only once every option
//!   has been read. An unknown signal is `'X': invalid signal`, said
//!   where it is met. Each is a usage error, status 1.
//! - **Sending.** The default is `TERM`. Signal 0 (`0`, `EXIT`) sends
//!   nothing and still asks whether it could. Every PID is tried; one
//!   that is not a number is `'X': invalid process id`, one the host
//!   refuses is `'X': REASON` in the host's words, and either makes the
//!   status 1 without stopping the rest. No PID at all is `no process
//!   ID specified`, a usage error. Nothing is printed on success.
//! - **Listing.** `-l` alone prints every name, `EXIT` first, one a
//!   line; `-t` alone prints the number, right-aligned in two columns,
//!   and the name. With operands each is answered in turn: `-l` prints
//!   the name for a number and the number for a name, `-t` the row. An
//!   operand that names no signal is reported and the rest are still
//!   answered; the status is then 1, and no `Try` line follows.
//! - Options permute unless `POSIXLY_CORRECT` is set, when the first
//!   operand ends them: `kill 12 -l` is then two PIDs.
//!
//! **What this `kill` cannot do, each refused, status 1, nothing sent:**
//!
//! - **A process group.** The ids `0`, `-1` and every negative id name
//!   a group or every process, and wolf's `os_signal_send` takes one
//!   process, an id above 0 (`[os.signal.send]`: "a group … is job
//!   control's, the next lane's"). Refused by name, where GNU sends.
//! - **A signal wolf has no meaning for**: real-time signals, and what
//!   only one host has (`STKFLT`, `POLL`, `PWR`; `EMT`, `INFO`). They
//!   are `invalid signal` here, by name and by number, where GNU would
//!   send or name them. So `-l` and `-t` alone print 29 lines, the 28
//!   signals wolf can send and `EXIT`, in the host's numeric order,
//!   where GNU prints every signal the host has.
//! - **A signal's description.** GNU's `-t` has a third column, the
//!   host's own words for the signal (`strsignal`: `Killed`, and
//!   `Killed: 9` on macOS). wolf has `os_error_text` for an error
//!   number and nothing for a signal's, and a table of one C library's
//!   words would be wrong on the other host. The column is left out.
//! - **Why a send failed, past the two reasons that have rows.**
//!   `not_found` is the host's `No such process` and `denied` its
//!   `Operation not permitted`; any other failure is `io`, and the os
//!   family leaves no error number behind (wolf-lang#682).

use bore

fn main() -> int {
    let args = env_args()
    let posix = !bore.permute()
    var out = bore.stdout()
    let table = bore.signal_table()
    // 0: send. 1: -l. 2: -t.
    var listing = 0
    var named = false
    var meaning = bore.signal_default()
    var operands = List[str]()
    var stopped = false
    var i = 0
    while i < args.len {
        let a = args[i]
        i += 1
        if stopped {
            (mut operands).push(a)
            continue
        }
        if a == "--" {
            stopped = true
            continue
        }
        if a.starts_with("--") {
            // One long option, read by the shared parser: alone first,
            // and with the next word only when it asks for one.
            var one = List[str]()
            (mut one).push(a)
            var p = bore.parse(long_options(), one, false)
            if p.error == "option '--signal' requires an argument" && i < args.len {
                (mut one).push(args[i])
                i += 1
                p = bore.parse(long_options(), one, false)
            }
            if !bore.ok(p) {
                return bore.usage_error("kill", p.error)
            }
            let k = p.keys[0]
            if k == "help" {
                bore.put(mut out, help()) else |_| {}
                return bore.finish(mut out, "kill", 0)
            }
            if k == "version" {
                bore.put(mut out, bore.version_text("kill")) else |_| {}
                return bore.finish(mut out, "kill", 0)
            }
            if k == "signal" {
                let spec = p.values[0]
                if named {
                    return bore.usage_error(
                        "kill",
                        "{bore.quote(spec)}: multiple signals specified",
                    )
                }
                let entry = bore.signal_entry(table, spec) else |_| {
                    return bore.usage_error("kill", "{bore.quote(spec)}: invalid signal")
                }
                meaning = bore.signal_meaning(table, entry)
                named = true
            } else {
                if listing != 0 {
                    return bore.usage_error("kill", "multiple -l or -t options specified")
                }
                listing = if k == "list" then 1 else 2
            }
            continue
        }
        if !a.starts_with("-") || a.len == 1 {
            (mut operands).push(a)
            if posix {
                stopped = true
            }
            continue
        }
        let first = letter_at(a, 1)
        if is_digit(first) && i != 1 {
            // Not the first argument, so a process group's id: option
            // reading stops here and what was permuted before it is
            // lost, as GNU loses it (the header).
            operands = List[str]()
            (mut operands).push(a)
            stopped = true
            continue
        }
        if is_digit(first) || is_upper(first) && first != "L" {
            let spec = a[1..]
            if named {
                return bore.usage_error("kill", "{bore.quote(spec)}: multiple signals specified")
            }
            let entry = bore.signal_entry(table, spec) else |_| {
                return bore.usage_error("kill", "{bore.quote(spec)}: invalid signal")
            }
            meaning = bore.signal_meaning(table, entry)
            named = true
            continue
        }
        var off = 1
        while off < a.len {
            let c = letter_at(a, off)
            off += c.len
            if c == "l" || c == "t" || c == "L" {
                if listing != 0 {
                    return bore.usage_error("kill", "multiple -l or -t options specified")
                }
                listing = if c == "l" then 1 else 2
            } else if c == "s" || c == "n" {
                var spec = ""
                if off < a.len {
                    spec = a[off..]
                    off = a.len
                } else if i < args.len {
                    spec = args[i]
                    i += 1
                } else {
                    return bore.usage_error("kill", "option requires an argument -- '{c}'")
                }
                if named {
                    return bore.usage_error(
                        "kill",
                        "{bore.quote(spec)}: multiple signals specified",
                    )
                }
                let entry = bore.signal_entry(table, spec) else |_| {
                    return bore.usage_error("kill", "{bore.quote(spec)}: invalid signal")
                }
                meaning = bore.signal_meaning(table, entry)
                named = true
            } else if is_digit(c) || is_upper(c) {
                // A signal's first character, where no signal can start.
                return bore.usage_error("kill", "invalid option -- {c}")
            } else {
                return bore.usage_error("kill", "invalid option -- '{c}'")
            }
        }
    }
    if listing != 0 && named {
        return bore.usage_error("kill", "cannot combine signal with -l or -t")
    }
    if listing != 0 {
        return list(mut out, table, operands, listing == 2)
    }
    if operands.len == 0 {
        return bore.usage_error("kill", "no process ID specified")
    }
    var status = 0
    // PLANT (bu23, reverted next): whatever signal was named, TERM is sent.
    if named && meaning != 0 {
        meaning = bore.signal_default()
    }
    for a in operands {
        let pid = bore.process_id(a) else |_| {
            bore.warn("kill", "{bore.quote(a)}: invalid process id")
            status = 1
            continue
        }
        if pid <= 0 {
            bore.warn(
                "kill",
                "{bore.quote(a)}: a process group cannot be signalled: wolf sends to one process, an id above 0 ([os.signal.send])",
            )
            status = 1
            continue
        }
        let why = send(pid, meaning)
        if why.len > 0 {
            // PLANT (bu23, reverted next): a failed send leaves the status 0.
            bore.warn("kill", "{bore.quote(a)}: {why}")
        }
    }
    bore.finish(mut out, "kill", status)
}

/// Send `meaning`'s signal to the process `pid`; `""`, or why the host
/// would not, in its own words where a row names the reason.
fn send(pid: int, meaning: int) -> str {
    var why = ""
    os_signal_send(pid, meaning) else |e| match e {
        not_found => {
            why = "No such process"
        },
        denied => {
            why = "Operation not permitted"
        },
        invalid => {
            why = "not a process id wolf can signal ([os.signal.send])"
        },
        unsupported => {
            why = "this host has no signals to send"
        },
        io => {
            why = "cannot be signalled, and wolf does not say why (wolf-lang#682)"
        },
    }
    why
}

/// `-l` and `-t`: the whole table, or an answer for each operand.
fn list(mut out: bore.Out, table: bore.SignalTable, operands: List[str], tabular: bool) -> int {
    var status = 0
    if operands.len == 0 {
        var i = -1
        while i < table.numbers.len {
            line(mut out, table, i, tabular, true)
            i += 1
        }
        return bore.finish(mut out, "kill", 0)
    }
    for a in operands {
        let entry = bore.signal_entry(table, a) else |_| {
            bore.warn("kill", "{bore.quote(a)}: invalid signal")
            status = 1
            continue
        }
        line(mut out, table, entry, tabular, bore.signal_is_number(a))
    }
    bore.finish(mut out, "kill", status)
}

/// One line of a list: the table's row, else the name (`by_name`) or
/// the number of entry `i`.
fn line(mut out: bore.Out, table: bore.SignalTable, i: int, tabular: bool, by_name: bool) {
    let number = bore.signal_number(table, i)
    let name = bore.signal_name(table, i)
    if tabular {
        bore.put(mut out, "{bore.space_padded(number, 2)} {name}\n") else |_| {}
    } else if by_name {
        bore.put(mut out, "{name}\n") else |_| {}
    } else {
        bore.put(mut out, "{number}\n") else |_| {}
    }
}

/// The character of `s` starting at byte `off`, as a `str`; `""` past
/// the end.
fn letter_at(s: str, off: int) -> str {
    if off >= s.len {
        return ""
    }
    for c in s[off..].chars() {
        return "{c}"
    }
    ""
}

/// The one byte of `c`, or -1 when it is not one byte.
fn code(c: str) -> int {
    if c.len != 1 {
        return -1
    }
    for b in c.bytes() {
        return b as int
    }
    -1
}

fn is_digit(c: str) -> bool {
    code(c) >= 48 && code(c) <= 57
}

fn is_upper(c: str) -> bool {
    code(c) >= 65 && code(c) <= 90
}

/// The long options, in the order GNU names them when a prefix is
/// ambiguous (`--=`).
fn long_options() -> List[bore.Opt] {
    var s = List[bore.Opt]()
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "list"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "signal"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "table"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "help"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "version"))
    s
}

fn help() -> str {
    """
    Usage: kill [-s SIGNAL | -SIGNAL] PID...
      or:  kill -l [SIGNAL]...
      or:  kill -t [SIGNAL]...
    Send a signal to each PID (TERM when none is named), or list signals.

      -s, --signal=SIGNAL, -SIGNAL
                    the signal to send, by name or by number
      -l, --list    print signal names, or turn each SIGNAL from a name
                    into its number and from a number into its name
      -t, --table   print each signal's number and name
          --help    print this help and exit
          --version print the version and exit

    SIGNAL is a name such as HUP or SIGHUP, in any case; a number such as
    1; or the exit status of a process a signal ended. The numbers are
    this host's. 0, or EXIT, sends nothing and reports whether it could.

    This kill knows the 28 signals wolf can send, every one POSIX names.
    It refuses a real-time signal and one only this host has; it refuses
    a process group (PID 0, -1 or any negative id); and -t leaves out the
    host's description of each signal.

    The status is 1 when a signal could not be sent or a SIGNAL names
    nothing. Your shell has a kill of its own, which is the one it runs
    unless this one is named by its path.

    boreutils: https://github.com/wolffe-lang/boreutils
    """
}
